.TH look "" "Command"
.SH NAME
look \- find matching lines in a sorted file
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBlook [\-df] \fIstring\fR [\fIfile\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
look -- find matching lines in a sorted file
Usage:	look [ -df ] string [ file ]
Options:
	-d	Dictionary ordering
	-f	Fold cases for comparison
If no 'file', look uses '/usr/dict/words' with -df option.
.HE
.B look
does a binary search of the sorted
.I file
and prints every line that begins with
.IR string .
If no
.I file
is given,
.B look
searches
.B /usr/dict/words
and behaves as if both
.B \-d
and
.B \-f
had been given.
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Dictionary order: while comparing,
.I string
and the file are considered a character at a time and only
alphanumerics and blanks participate; all other characters are
skipped over.
.IP \fB\-f\fR
Fold case: both
.I string
and the lines read from the file have their lower-case letters
converted to upper case before being compared, so the search is
case-insensitive.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 20n
\fB/usr/dict/words\fR	default dictionary file
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B sort
.SH NOTES
.I string
and
.I file
should already be sorted in the same order that
.B \-d
and
.B \-f
select; if the file was not sorted with matching
.B sort
options,
.B look
may fail to find lines that are actually present.
